   <euvoc:XlNote rdf:about="http://publications.europa.eu/resource/authority/file-type/b6e4ac852b44b484964cec3646eea012">
      <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://publications.europa.eu/ontology/euvoc#XlNote"/>
      <euvoc:startDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2013-11-27</euvoc:startDate>
      <dct:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2018-02-26</dct:created>
      <rdf:value xml:lang="en">RSS – Rich Site Summary; originally RDF Site Summary; often called Really Simple Syndication – is a type of web feed which allows users to access updates to online content in a standardized, computer-readable format. These feeds can, for example, allow a user to keep track of many different websites in a single news aggregator. Websites usually use RSS feeds to publish frequently updated information, such as blog entries, news headlines, audio, video. An RSS document (called ‘feed’, ‘web feed’ or ‘channel’) includes full or summarized text and metadata.</rdf:value>
      <dct:creator>Wikipedia</dct:creator>
      <euvoc:xlNote xml:lang="en">RSS – Rich Site Summary; originally RDF Site Summary; often called Really Simple Syndication – is a type of web feed which allows users to access updates to online content in a standardized, computer-readable format. These feeds can, for example, allow a user to keep track of many different websites in a single news aggregator. Websites usually use RSS feeds to publish frequently updated information, such as blog entries, news headlines, audio, video. An RSS document (called ‘feed’, ‘web feed’ or ‘channel’) includes full or summarized text and metadata.</euvoc:xlNote>
   </euvoc:XlNote>
